2. Algorithm 1: Document Input and Validation
Input: Uploaded file or user-provided URL.
Method: 1. Receive the file or URL. 2. Check file extension, MIME type, file size, and basic integrity. 3. Reject unsupported or unsafe files. 4. Identify whether the input is PDF, image, DOCX, or supported web content. 5. Store the file securely for temporary processing. 6. Pass it to the appropriate extraction module.
Output: Validated document ready for processing.
3. Algorithm 2: Text Extraction and OCR
Input: Validated document.
Method: 1. If the PDF contains selectable text, extract text and page information using a PDF parser. 2. If the page is scanned or image-based, render the page as an image. 3. Run OCR on the image. 4. Retain page numbers and, where available, text positions or bounding boxes. 5. Combine extracted text in document order. 6. Flag low-confidence or unreadable text for user review.
Output: Extracted text and available layout information.
Important: OCR is not guaranteed to read every scan correctly. Low-quality images, handwriting, tables, and unusual fonts may require correction.
4. Algorithm 3: Dynamic Form Field Detection
Input: Extracted document text and layout.
Method: 1. Split the document into sections and candidate questions. 2. Detect labels, instructions, blank lines, checkboxes, tables, and existing PDF form fields. 3. Classify each candidate field, for example: - Name - Date - Email - Phone - Address - Education - Number/percentage - Checkbox - Long-text answer 4. Detect whether the field appears required or optional from nearby instructions and document cues. 5. Infer the expected answer format where possible. 6. Create a structured field object.
Example structured field:
{
  "field_id": "applicant_full_name",
  "label": "Name of the Applicant",
  "field_type": "text",
  "required": true,
  "expected_language": "English",
  "source_page": 1,
  "confidence": 0.94
}
The confidence value must come from the actual extraction or classification process. Do not use a made-up confidence score.
Output: Dynamic list of fields for user confirmation.
5. Algorithm 4: Telugu Question Generation
Input: A detected field label and its instructions.
Method: 1. Read the field label and supporting instructions. 2. Translate or explain the question in simple Telugu. 3. Preserve important names, numbers, dates, and technical terms. 4. Generate a short question that asks only for the required information. 5. Send the question to Telugu text-to-speech. 6. Display the text so the user can read it as well.
Example:
Form label: Enter your date of birth
Telugu question: మీ పుట్టిన తేదీ చెప్పండి.
Expected answer: Date
6. Algorithm 5: Telugu Speech Recognition
Input: User's recorded Telugu audio.
Method: 1. Request microphone permission. 2. Record audio only after the user starts the microphone. 3. Send audio to the configured speech-recognition engine. 4. Receive the recognised text. 5. Display the transcript to the user. 6. Allow the user to correct the transcript. 7. Continue only after the answer is confirmed or sufficiently clear.
Output: Telugu text transcript.
7. Algorithm 6: Answer Extraction and Translation
Input: User's typed answer or speech transcript, plus target field details.
Method: 1. Identify the information requested by the current field. 2. Extract the relevant value from the user's response. 3. Remove conversational wording without changing the meaning. 4. Determine the language required by the target field. 5. If translation is needed, translate the extracted value. 6. Keep the original answer available for review. 7. Ask a clarification question if the value is ambiguous.
Example:
User says: నా పేరు బాబి కొప్పనాతి
Extracted value: బాబి కొప్పనాతి
English rendering (if the user confirms this spelling): Babi Koppanathi
Names must not be transliterated or altered without user confirmation when spelling matters.
8. Algorithm 7: Semantic Field Mapping
Input: Extracted answer, current question, and detected field list.
Method: 1. Identify the meaning of the current question. 2. Compare it with candidate field labels and descriptions. 3. Use exact label matching first when reliable. 4. Otherwise use semantic similarity or an AI classification model. 5. Check the expected data type and nearby context. 6. Select the best candidate only if confidence meets the configured threshold. 7. If multiple fields are plausible, ask the user to choose. 8. Show the proposed mapping before committing it.
Example:
Detected form label    Information category
Applicant Name         Full name Student's Name         Full name Date of Birth          DOB Name of Institution    College/Institution Aggregate Percentage   Percentage
Output: A proposed mapping between user answers and document fields.
9. Algorithm 8: Field Validation
Input: Proposed field value and field rules.
Method: 1. Check whether required information is present. 2. Check data type and format. 3. Validate email syntax, date format, and numeric values where applicable. 4. Check configured length or range constraints. 5. If invalid, explain the issue in Telugu and request correction. 6. Do not silently modify important personal information.
Output: Validated value or a correction request.
10. Algorithm 9: User Review
Input: All mapped and validated answers.
Method: 1. Display every detected field and proposed answer. 2. Highlight missing, uncertain, or invalid fields. 3. Allow the user to edit any value. 4. Re-run validation after edits. 5. Require confirmation before generating the final document.
Output: User-approved set of answers.
11. Algorithm 10: PDF Filling and Generation
Input: Original document, field map, and approved answers.
Method: 1. Check whether the PDF has editable AcroForm fields. 2. If editable fields exist, map values to those fields. 3. If no editable fields exist, use page layout and detected positions to place text, or generate a new completed document. 4. Embed or use fonts that support Telugu Unicode. 5. Preserve the original layout as far as possible. 6. Generate the output PDF. 7. Reopen and verify that the generated file is readable. 8. Show the preview and provide a download option.
Output: Completed document for user review.
12. Simplified Pseudocode
START

Receive uploaded document or supported URL

Validate input
IF input is unsupported:
    Show error
    STOP

Extract document text and layout

IF document is scanned or image-based:
    Run OCR

Detect questions and form fields
Create dynamic field list
Show fields for confirmation

FOR each required field:
    Generate Telugu question
    Speak question or display text

    Receive voice or typed answer

    IF voice answer:
        Convert speech to text

    Extract answer value

    IF translation is required:
        Translate value
        Preserve original answer

    Map answer to correct field

    Validate answer

    IF answer is missing, invalid, or ambiguous:
        Ask user to correct or clarify
    ELSE:
        Save proposed field value

Show completed form for review
Allow edits
Validate edited values

IF user approves:
    Fill supported PDF or generate completed document
    Verify generated PDF
    Display preview and download option

END
13. Key Methods Used
Method                  Purpose
PDF/DOCX parsing        Read digital documents OCR                     Extract text from scanned documents NLP                     Understand questions and answers Text classification     Identify field categories Semantic similarity     Match different labels with similar meanings Speech-to-Text          Convert Telugu speech into text Text-to-Speech          Ask questions using Telugu audio Machine translation     Convert Telugu and English text when needed Rule-based validation   Check answer formats and required values PDF form filling        Place answers into supported PDF fields
14. Accuracy and Error Handling
Ask the user when a question is unclear.
Show OCR text that may be incorrect.
Never guess missing personal details.
Never silently change names, dates, marks, or qualifications.
Preserve the original answer when translation is used.
Require user review before final document generation.
Keep external website submission under the user's control.
